WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.470
Cells are the smallest living units of
an organism.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.439
All cells have three things in
common

00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:18.559
no matter what type of cell they are.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:25.000
All cells have a cell membrane which separates the
inside the cell from its environment,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.000
cytoplasm, which is a jelly-like fluid,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.600
and DNA which is the cell's genetic
material.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.780
There are two broad categories of cells.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.000
The first category is eukaryotic cells.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.790
They have organelles

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.990
which include the nucleus and other
special parts.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.720
Eukaryotic cells are more advanced,
complex cells

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.400
such as those found in plants and
animals.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:00.940
The second category is prokaryotic
cells.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.780
They don't have a nucleus or membrane
enclosed organelles.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.560
They do have genetic material but it's
not contained within a nucleus.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.080
Prokaryotic cells are always one celled,

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:16.530
or unicellular organisms, such as
bacteria.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.160
So what are organelles?

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.000
Organelle means "little organ."

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.700
Organelles are the specialized parts of a cell that have unique jobs to perform.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.960
Let's start with the nucleus, the
control center of the cell.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.570
The nucleus contains DNA or genetic
material.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.060
DNA dictates what the cell is going
to do

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.160
and how it's going to do it.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.939
Chromatin the tangled, spread out form of DNA
found inside the nuclear membrane.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.340
When a cell is ready to divide

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.500
DNA condenses into structures known as
chromosomes.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.100
The nucleus also contains a nucleolus,

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.460
which is a structure where ribosomes
are made.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.140
After ribosomes leave the nucleus

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.880
they will have the important job  of "synthesizing",

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.200
or making, proteins.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.910
Outside the nucleus the ribosomes and
the rest of the organelles

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.760
float around in cytoplasm, which is the
jelly-like substance.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.700
Ribosomes may wander freely within
the cytoplasm

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:48.080
or attach to the endoplasmic
reticulum, sometimes abbreviated as ER.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.680
There are two types of ER:

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.150
rough ER has ribosomes attached to it

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.080
and smooth ER doesn't have
ribosomes attached to it.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.540
The endoplasmic reticulum

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.330
is a membrane enclosed passageway for
transporting materials

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.540
such as the proteins synthesized by
ribosomes.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.100
Proteins and other materials

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.850
emerge from the endoplasmic reticulum

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.610
in small vesicles

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.810
where the Golgi apparatus, sometimes called the Golgi body

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.470
receives them.

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:31.010
As proteins move through the Golgi body
they're customized

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.210
into forms that the cell can use.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:40.230
The Golgi body does this by folding the
proteins into usable shapes.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.550
or adding other materials on to them

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:44.750
such as lipids

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.140
or carbohydrates

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.300
Vacuoles are sac-like structures that store
different materials.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.150
Here, in this plant cell, the central vacuole stores water.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.140
Going back to the animal cell,

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.180
you'll see an organelle called a lysosome.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.400
Lysosomes are the garbage collectors

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.360
that take in damaged or worn out cell parts.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:19.440
They are filled with enzymes that break
down this cellular debris.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.900
The mitochondrion in is an organelle
that is the powerhouse for both

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.710
animal and plant cells.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.590
During a process called cellular respiration

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:34.620
the mitochondria make ATP molecules

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.940
that provide the energy for all the
cells activities.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.900
Cells that need more energy

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:43.740
have more mitochondria.

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:49.600
Meanwhile the cell maintains its shape

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.400
through a cytoskeleton.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.700
The cytoskeleton includes the thread-like microfilaments

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.460
which are made of protein

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:02.440
and microtubules which are thin hollow tubes

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:06.420
Some organisms

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:10.460
such as plans that are photoautotrophic

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.940
meaning they capture sunlight for energy

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.340
have cells with an organelle called
a chloroplast.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.560
The chloroplast is where
photosynthesis happens

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:26.810
It's green because it has a green
pigment called

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:28.260
chlorophyll.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.830
Plant cells also have a cell wall
outside of their cell membranes

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:38.040
that shape, support, and protect the plant cell.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.020
Animal cells never have a cell wall

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.800
There are many other unique
structures that only some cells have.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.000
Here are just a few.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:56.000
In humans, for example, the respiratory tract is lined with cells that have cilia.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.440
These are microscopic
hair-like projections

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.500
that can move in waves.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:08.140
This feature helps trap inhaled particles in the air and expels them when you cough.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:15.700
Another unique feature in some cells is flagella.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.049
Some bacteria have flagella.

00:06:18.049 --> 00:06:23.779
A flagellum is like a little tail that
can help a cell move or propel itself.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.820
The only human cell that has a flagellum

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.600
is a sperm cell.

00:06:31.549 --> 00:06:33.469
In summary remember:

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:40.479
eukaryotic cells are plant and animal cells with a nucleus and membrane-enclosed organelles

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:47.820
While prokaryotic cells are unicellular organisms without these things.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:54.860
All cells have a cell membrane, cytoplasm and genetic material.

00:06:56.799 --> 00:06:59.839
And even though only plant cells have
chloroplasts

00:06:59.839 --> 00:07:03.159
both plant and animal cells have mitochondria.

